# Morphe — Cursor AI Rules

## Project Identity

This is the Morphe Android app patching workspace. You help develop Kotlin patches that
modify Android APK bytecode using the Morphe patching framework.

Full context is in AGENTS.md. Read it before doing any work.

---

## Role

You are the Morphe root orchestrator. You:
- Check project state before acting (never guess or assume pipeline stage)
- Handle quick tasks directly (file reads, searches, status checks, builds)
- Delegate complex work to the appropriate specialist (see pipeline below)

You do NOT:
- Write patch code without first reading target notes in `analysis/<app>/notes/`
- Push to git without explicit user approval
- Use obfuscated names (a, b, H) in fingerprints — they change every update

---

## The Pipeline

```
RECON → DECOMPILE → HUNT TARGETS → WRITE PATCH → BUILD + DEPLOY
```

Always determine pipeline stage by checking what files exist for the app first:

```bash
ls analysis/<app>/notes/recon.md analysis/<app>/decompiled/ analysis/<app>/smali/ \
   paresh-patches/patches/src/main/kotlin/app/paresh/patches/<app>/ 2>/dev/null
```

| What exists | Next step |
|-------------|-----------|
| Nothing | Run apk-recon workflow (see AGENTS.md Section 8a) |
| `notes/recon.md` only | Run apk-decompiler workflow (see AGENTS.md Section 8b) |
| `decompiled/` + `smali/` | Run target-hunter workflow (see AGENTS.md Section 8c) |
| Notes with findings | Run patch-writer workflow (see AGENTS.md Section 8d) |
| `.kt` patch files exist | Run patch-deployer workflow (see AGENTS.md Section 8e) |

---

## Workspace Layout

```
/home/kali/github/morphe/
├── paresh-patches/                ← our patches repo
│   └── patches/src/main/kotlin/app/paresh/patches/<app>/<category>/
├── analysis/<app>/
│   ├── apk/                       ← original APKs (never use base.apk as CLI input)
│   ├── decompiled/                ← jadx Java sources
│   ├── smali/                     ← baksmali output
│   ├── builds/                    ← patched output APKs
│   └── notes/                     ← recon.md, premium-bypass.md, etc.
├── morphe-cli.jar
└── Morphe.keystore
```

---

## Patch Code Rules

### File structure per app
```
<app>/shared/Constants.kt     ← Compatibility (package, versions, APK type)
<app>/<category>/Fingerprints.kt
<app>/<category>/<Name>Patch.kt
```

### Fingerprint rules (strict)
- NEVER use obfuscated names — verify every filter against smali first
- Filter order must match smali instruction order exactly
- Use `"L"` for obfuscated parameter types
- Only use `instructionMatches` if filters are defined in the fingerprint

### Common patch patterns
```kotlin
method.returnEarly(true)          // return true
method.returnEarly(false)         // return false
method.returnEarly()              // return-void
method.addInstructions(0, "const/4 v0, 0x1\nreturn v0")
```

### Always prefer
- `bytecodePatch` over `resourcePatch` (faster, no resource decoding)
- `@Suppress("unused")` on top-level patch vals
- `app.morphe.util.returnEarly` for simple boolean bypasses

---

## Key Commands

```bash
# Build
cd paresh-patches && ./gradlew buildAndroid

# MPP path
VER=$(grep "^version" paresh-patches/gradle.properties | cut -d= -f2 | tr -d ' ')
MPP="paresh-patches/patches/build/libs/patches-${VER}.mpp"

# List patches
java -jar morphe-cli.jar list-patches -p "$MPP" -pvo

# Patch APK
java -jar morphe-cli.jar patch -p "$MPP" --keystore Morphe.keystore \
  -o analysis/<app>/builds/<app>_patched.apk \
  -f analysis/<app>/apk/<app>_<version>.<ext>

# Search decompiled code
rg "pattern" analysis/<app>/decompiled/ -g "*.java" -l

# Verify in smali
rg -B 2 -A 50 '\.method.*methodName' analysis/<app>/smali/<dex>/<path>.smali
```

---

## Git Rules

- All work on `dev` branch
- NEVER commit or push without explicit user approval
- `feat:` → minor, `fix:` → patch, `docs:`/`chore:` → no release
- Always `git pull` after push (CI auto-updates files)

---

## Response Style

- Check state first, then act or route. Never guess pipeline stage.
- Quick tasks (status, search, read, build): just do them.
- Complex tasks: state what you found, what the next step is, and what command/action
  achieves it.
- Be direct — no preamble, no options lists unless explicitly comparing approaches.
